gn sovereigns for human rights and law of war violations, or if the expropriation exception only applies to property claims.\n  - id: 22-859, name: SEC v. Jarkesy, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether Congress has the authority to allow administrative agencies to impose civil penalties and conduct adjudications without violating the Seventh Amendment or the nondelegation doctrine, as seen in the case of SEC v. Jarkesy.\n  - id: 20-603, name: Torres v. Texas Dept. of Public Safety, description: The Constitution granted Congress the power to raise and support Armies, which includes authorizing lawsuits against states, to ensure national survival. This power is unique and essential for the federal government\'s preeminent national defense function, particularly in light of modern military needs and USERRA protections.\n  - id: 21-1170, name: Ciminelli v. United State, description: The Supreme Court is considering a case where the government\'s theory of wire fraud, known as the right-to-control doctrine, has been deemed invalid. The government is now proposing a new theory of fraud, but this Court has never accepted it and the defendant was not convicted based on this theory. The issue is whether to adopt this new theory, which would expand federal law and potentially violate principles of federalism.\n  - id: 19-465, name: Chiafalo v. Washington, description: The case, Chiafalo v. Washington, centers on whether states have the power to control how electors vote in presidential elections, with the Constitution\'s design and language being key factors in determining this issue.\n  - id: 22-210, name: Dupree v. Younger, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether a party must reargue a legal issue at trial to preserve it for appeal, or if the issue is automatically preserved after a summary judgment ruling.\n  - id: 21-1052, name: U.S., ex rel. Polansky v. Executive Health, description: The government\'s authority to dismiss a False Claims Act case after declining to proceed is at issue in this Supreme Court case, with the government arguing it has such authority and the relator disagreeing.\n  - id: 19-715, name: Trump v. Mazars USA, LLP, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether Congress has the authority to subpoena personal records of a sitting President, with implications for the balance of power between the legislative and executive branches.\n  - id: 22-846, name: Dept. of Agric. Rural Dev. v. Kirtz, description: The Supreme Court is deciding whether Congress clearly gave up the government\'s immunity from lawsuits when it changed the Fair Credit Reporting Act in 1996 to allow people who break its rules to be held liable for money damages.\n  - id: 24-354, name: FCC v. Consumers\' Research, description: The case concerns whether Congress has delegated too much authority to the Federal Communications Commission in setting a fee for telecommunications carriers, with some arguing that this delegation violates constitutional limits.\n  - id: 21-12, name: Federal Election Commission v. Ted Cruz for Senate, description: The case concerns the constitutionality of a statutory loan repayment limit for election campaigns, with Appellees arguing that it should be reversed and the district court\'s judgment upheld.\n  - id: 21-86, name: Axon Enterprise, Inc. v. FTC, description: The Supreme Court is considering whether Congress has the authority to allow district courts to hear constitutional claims related to agency officials\' removal and access to the courts, despite a separate grant of jurisdiction to appeals courts for unrelated orders.\n\nFollow these rules:\n- Extract parameters strictly according to the schema.\n- Apply the extraction instructions above.\n- Use context only when the user refers indirectly (e.g., "it", "that one").\n- If a parameter cannot be extracted, set it to null.\n- Return ONLY valid JSON. No commentary.\n\nReturn JSON in this format:\n{\n    "parameters": {\n        <param_name>: <value or null>\n    },\n    "reasoning": "<brief explanation>"\n}\n',)
